1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,907
With that, we have the base usage left. And

2
00:00:02,907 --> 00:00:04,449
obviously you can open it, you can type a

3
00:00:04,449 --> 00:00:06,392
prompt, I know, but there's a bit more to

4
00:00:06,392 --> 00:00:06,617
that.

5
00:00:07,324 --> 00:00:08,818
For one, it's worth noting that there are

6
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:10,970
many slash commands which you can start by

7
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:13,508
simply typing slash and then some command.

8
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:15,837
And these change over time. New ones are

9
00:00:15,837 --> 00:00:18,166
added, some are deprecated. One important

10
00:00:18,166 --> 00:00:19,596
one, for example, is the clear command,

11
00:00:19,708 --> 00:00:21,781
which I just ran, which I can run again. The

12
00:00:21,781 --> 00:00:23,981
clear command simply clears the current

13
00:00:23,981 --> 00:00:26,629
session and important, the context window

14
00:00:26,694 --> 00:00:28,938
related to the session so that you can start

15
00:00:28,938 --> 00:00:31,871
a new clean session. Of course, that session

16
00:00:31,887 --> 00:00:33,795
and the AI running in there won't know

17
00:00:33,827 --> 00:00:35,606
anything about the previous one, but you

18
00:00:35,606 --> 00:00:37,434
typically want that when you clear a

19
00:00:37,434 --> 00:00:39,918
session. You want to free up the context

20
00:00:39,918 --> 00:00:42,259
memory and start fresh. This can, for

21
00:00:42,259 --> 00:00:44,808
example, also be helpful if the AI agent got

22
00:00:44,808 --> 00:00:47,533
stuck and can't figure out or fix a problem,

23
00:00:47,853 --> 00:00:49,681
or if you're simply working on a different

24
00:00:49,681 --> 00:00:50,017
problem.

25
00:00:50,643 --> 00:00:52,598
Obviously, you can also just start a new

26
00:00:52,598 --> 00:00:54,746
session by opening a new terminal window and

27
00:00:54,746 --> 00:00:57,119
starting Claude in there too. You can have

28
00:00:57,215 --> 00:00:58,962
multiple Claude Code sessions run in

29
00:00:58,994 --> 00:01:01,237
parallel. You can work on the same project

30
00:01:01,237 --> 00:01:02,808
with multiple Claude Code sessions.

31
00:01:03,481 --> 00:01:05,789
You just wanna steer it such that the agents

32
00:01:05,789 --> 00:01:08,016
don't work against each other in the same

33
00:01:08,128 --> 00:01:10,212
file, for example. But hey, that's why

34
00:01:10,212 --> 00:01:12,119
you're a developer. You can control what

35
00:01:12,199 --> 00:01:14,186
you're doing with those AI assistants.

36
00:01:14,859 --> 00:01:16,750
Now, there are many other useful tools in

37
00:01:16,782 --> 00:01:19,763
there too. For example, context tells you

38
00:01:20,372 --> 00:01:23,240
how the context window for this session is

39
00:01:23,353 --> 00:01:25,740
currently being used or occupied. And since

40
00:01:25,821 --> 00:01:29,074
I just cleared it, it's almost empty. Well,

41
00:01:29,507 --> 00:01:33,294
kind of. Interestingly enough, 20,000 of the

42
00:01:33,294 --> 00:01:36,631
200,000 tokens that are available for Claude

43
00:01:36,663 --> 00:01:38,717
Code Opus, the model I'm using here where

44
00:01:38,717 --> 00:01:41,381
I'm recording this, are already occupied by

45
00:01:41,397 --> 00:01:41,814
default.

46
00:01:42,664 --> 00:01:44,766
And you see that a part of that is the

47
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:47,109
system prompt that was set up by the Claude

48
00:01:47,109 --> 00:01:50,158
Code engineers and Anthropic. A decent chunk

49
00:01:50,222 --> 00:01:53,415
is occupied by system tools. So tool

50
00:01:53,415 --> 00:01:55,966
descriptions that are exposed to Claude Code

51
00:01:56,062 --> 00:01:58,212
to give it some hints on when to use which

52
00:01:58,325 --> 00:02:00,555
tool. And we'll get back to that.

53
00:02:01,549 --> 00:02:04,946
Some small part is occupied by MCP tools

54
00:02:04,978 --> 00:02:06,949
because I already added some MCP servers,

55
00:02:07,061 --> 00:02:08,663
but I'll also get back to that.

56
00:02:09,288 --> 00:02:11,115
And well, you see the distribution, but you

57
00:02:11,211 --> 00:02:13,438
also see there's a lot of free space.

58
00:02:14,015 --> 00:02:16,194
Also interesting is this auto-compact

59
00:02:16,194 --> 00:02:18,326
buffer, and we'll get back to compaction,

60
00:02:18,438 --> 00:02:20,585
but in the end, it's there to ensure that

61
00:02:20,585 --> 00:02:22,716
when you reach the limits of the context

62
00:02:22,764 --> 00:02:24,094
window for your session,

63
00:02:24,831 --> 00:02:27,844
Claude code will automatically compact it by

64
00:02:28,052 --> 00:02:30,103
generating a summary of the work that was

65
00:02:30,215 --> 00:02:31,898
done and the work that still needs to be

66
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:34,253
done so that it can throw away

67
00:02:35,167 --> 00:02:37,730
all the old context and just keep that

68
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:40,374
summary, that compaction summary, and

69
00:02:40,390 --> 00:02:42,922
continue based on that. And since that

70
00:02:43,195 --> 00:02:46,207
compaction generating that summary requires

71
00:02:46,976 --> 00:02:47,794
some tokens too,

72
00:02:48,386 --> 00:02:51,030
there is this reserved auto-compact buffer

73
00:02:51,511 --> 00:02:53,498
which you can't use, which is always

74
00:02:53,674 --> 00:02:55,568
reserved. Also interesting is the

75
00:02:56,339 --> 00:02:59,519
/usage tab, which shows you your remaining

76
00:02:59,856 --> 00:03:00,258
usage

77
00:03:01,077 --> 00:03:04,643
per your Claude Code plan so that you can

78
00:03:04,691 --> 00:03:07,261
see how hard you can go on Claude Code or if

79
00:03:07,261 --> 00:03:10,618
you should maybe decelerate and write more

80
00:03:10,618 --> 00:03:11,646
code from hand again

81
00:03:12,465 --> 00:03:14,730
or maybe pay extra for the extra usage.
